# Security headers for agent.buildclose.app (the chatbot origin).
#
# NOTE ON FORMAT: Netlify reads this file as PLAINTEXT. It is NOT JavaScript.
# A previous revision was written as an ES module (`export const headers = \`...\``)
# and placed at apps/_headers — outside every publish directory — so none of these
# policies were ever served. Requirement 3 (chat separate from dashboard) was
# resting on headers that never left the repository. Keep this file plaintext and
# keep it inside the publish dir it applies to.
#
# The compensating controls here matter: this origin is framable by arbitrary
# customer pages by design. It holds no cookies, keeps its session in memory only,
# and every request is authorised by a proposal token rather than ambient
# credentials — so being framed grants an attacker nothing.

/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: no-referrer
  Permissions-Policy: geolocation=(), microphone=(), camera=(), payment=()

# The loader is fetched cross-origin by customer pages, so it must be CORS-open.
# It carries no secrets: the proposal token is passed by the customer's own HTML
# attribute and verified server-side on every call.
/widget.js
  Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=300, stale-while-revalidate=86400
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: no-referrer

# The framed chat app. frame-ancestors is intentionally broad because the entire
# product is "be embedded in the customer's proposal page". connect-src is pinned
# to the Cloud Functions host so a compromised script cannot exfiltrate elsewhere.
#
# Both /embed and /embed/* are listed: netlify.toml rewrites /embed to
# /embed/index.html, and header rules are matched against the request path. Listing
# only one form risks the CSP silently not applying to the actual document, which
# would leave the surface framable by any origin with no frame-ancestors at all.
#
# Do NOT add a blanket `X-Frame-Options: DENY` under `/*` anywhere in this file.
# Netlify merges every matching rule, so a wildcard DENY would also apply here and
# silently block the iframe — the CSP frame-ancestors directive below is the
# precise control, and a wildcard XFO would override it.
/embed
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; connect-src 'self' https://us-central1-buildclose-d4399.cloudfunctions.net https://*.cloudfunctions.net https://*.run.app; img-src 'self' data:; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; script-src 'self'; frame-ancestors https:; base-uri 'none'; form-action 'self'
  Cache-Control: no-store
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: no-referrer

/embed/*
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; connect-src 'self' https://us-central1-buildclose-d4399.cloudfunctions.net https://*.cloudfunctions.net https://*.run.app; img-src 'self' data:; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; script-src 'self'; frame-ancestors https:; base-uri 'none'; form-action 'self'
  Cache-Control: no-store
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: no-referrer
